# Project Standards

Apply these project rules (derived from `.agents/skills`):

## React web performance (vercel-react-best-practices)
- Prefer parallel async work (`Promise.all`) and avoid request waterfalls.
- Minimize bundle cost: avoid barrel imports for heavy libs; favor direct imports and lazy load heavy UI/routes.
- Prefer derived state over effect-synced state; use stable dependencies and functional updates.
- Keep render cheap: avoid unnecessary memo/effect/useCallback; optimize only where profiling or obvious hot paths show need.
- Preserve server/API envelope assumptions and avoid introducing client-side data-shape drift.

## React composition architecture (vercel-composition-patterns)
- Avoid boolean-prop proliferation; prefer explicit variants or compound components.
- Use composition over render-prop bloat for reusable UI.
- Keep state ownership clear and colocated; lift state only when siblings need shared control.
- Separate interface from implementation details in shared component/state providers.

## Web UI quality baseline (web-design-guidelines)
- For UI/UX review tasks, fetch and follow latest web interface guidelines before proposing fixes.
- Prioritize accessibility, clarity, and consistent interaction/feedback patterns.

## React Native guidance (vercel-react-native-skills)
- Apply only when editing React Native/Expo code paths.
- For this repo (web-first), ignore RN-specific rules unless user explicitly asks for mobile modules.

## Implementation discipline
- Reuse existing project patterns/components before adding new abstractions.
- Keep changes small, production-safe, and verifiable with build/lint.
- Update i18n keys when introducing user-facing text.
